1 NAME Holika (Sinhika)
2 GIVN Holika (Sinhika)
1 SEX F
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Origin of Holika Dahan 
2 CONT
2 CONT For many traditions in Hinduism, Holi celebrates the death of Holika in order to save Prahlad and we see where Holi gets its name. The night before Holi, pyres are burnt in North India in keeping with this tradition. It should als
2 CONC o be noted that in some parts of India the day is actually called Holika[citation needed]. There are other activities associated with the story of Prahlad, but the burning of Holika is the one that we can most directly associate w
2 CONC ith Holi. The story as a whole is testament to the power of devotion (bhakta) over the evil represented by King Hiranyakashyapu, as Prahlad never lost his faith. The burning of Holika is the most common mythological explanation fo
2 CONC r the celebration of Holi. In different parts of India varying reasons are given for Holika's death: Vishnu intervened and hence Holika was burnt. Brahma had given Holika the power on the understanding that it can never be used to
2 CONC  harm anyone. Holika was good and it was her clothes that gave her the power. Knowing the evil about to be done to Prahlad, she gave these to the boy to save him, sacrificing herself.[7] When Holika sat on the pyre, she donned her
2 CONC  flame-shielding shawl and sat Prahlad down on her lap. When the fire was lit, Prahlad began praying to Vishnu, who summoned a gust of wind that blew the shawl off Holika and onto Prahlad, saving him and letting her burn to death.
2 CONC [8] 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holika 
