1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Narakasura 	
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Krishna and Satyabhama fighting Narakasura's armies -Painting from the Metropolitan Museum In Hindu beliefs, Narakasura or Narakais the asura son of the earth goddessBhudevi (Bhumi) and Lord Vishnu in hisVaraha (boar) Avatar (inca
2 CONC rnation). In other sources, he is the son of the asuraHiranyaksha. He is said to have established the kingdom of Pragjyotishain Assam after overthrowing the last of theDanava king Ghatakasura. It was foretold that he would be dest
2 CONC royed by a later incarnation of Vishnu. His mother, the earth, sought the boon from Vishnu that her son should have a long life, and that he should be all powerful. Vishnu granted these boons. The legend of Narakasura is important
2 CONC  in the history of Assam since Narakasura is cited as the progenitor of many dynasties that ruled Kamarupa in historical times. A hill, to the south ofGuwahati is named after him. He is also associated with the myth of the shakta 
2 CONC goddess and place of worship Kamakhya. The legend[edit]
2 CONT
2 CONT Krishna Cleaves the Demon Narakasura with his Discus Narakasura became evil, in association with anotherAsura named Banasura. Drunk with power, as he knew himself to be unrivalled in prowess, he brought all the kingdoms on earth u
2 CONC nder his control. Next, he turned his eyes towards Swargaloka. Even the mighty Indracould not withstand the assault of this son of Earth and had to flee the heavens. Narakasura had become the overlord of both the heavens and earth
2 CONC . Addicted to power, he stole the earrings of Aditi, the heavenly mother goddess, and usurped some of her territory, while also kidnapping 16000 women. All the Devas, led by Indra went to Vishnu, to ask him to deliver them from Na
2 CONC rakasura. Vishnu promised them that he would attend to this matter, when he would be incarnated as Krishna. As promised to Mother Earth, Narakasura was allowed to enjoy a long reign. At last Vishnu was born as Krishna. Aditi, who 
2 CONC was a relative of Krishna's wifeSatyabhama (believed to be an Avatar of Bhudevi - Narakasura' mother), approached Satyabhama for help. When Satyabhama heard of the Narakasura's ill treatment of women and his behaviour with Aditi, 
2 CONC she was enraged. Satyabhama approached Lord Krishna for permission to wage a war against Narakasura. As promised to the Devas and Aditi, Krishna attacked the great fortress of Narakasura, riding his mount Garuda with wife Satyabha
2 CONC ma. The battle was furiously fought. Narakasura possessed 11 Akshauhini (a division of the army), that he unleashed on Krishna. However, the Lord slew them all with little effort. Krishna also killed Mura, Narakasura's general. Th
2 CONC us Krishna is called 'Murāri'(the enemy of Mura). In desperation, Narakasura launched his great weapon, sataghini (a thunderbolt) on Krishna. However, it made no impact whatsoever on Krishna. At last, when Narakasura tried to kill
2 CONC  the Lord with a trident, Krishna beheaded him with hisSudarshana Chakra (discus). Before dying, the Asura requested a boon that his death anniversary should be celebrated by all people on earth. This day is celebrated as 'Naraka 
2 CONC Chaturdashi' - the second day of Diwali.
2 CONT
2 CONT Goddess of earth gives the Adithi's Kundalas to Krishna
2 CONT
2 CONT This section may need to be rewritten entirely to comply with Wikipedia's quality standards, as The following two paragraphs need reliable citation as well as copy-rewriting to correct semantic inconsistencies.. You can help. The 
2 CONC discussion pagemay contain suggestions. (May 2009) In another version, Narakasura had gained a boon fromBrahma that he would die only in the hands of his mother. On the day of the war, Satyabhama with Krishna fought Narakasura bra
2 CONC vely, but she was no match to his trained skills. After a few days, when Narakasura got a chance, he took aim at Krishna, hurting him lightly. Krishna fainted in a preordained, divine plan adopted to empower Satyabhama. As expecte
2 CONC d seeing this, Krishna being hurt, Satyabhama was furious. She doubled her attack on the demon king and finally killed him. Before Narakasura's death, he requested a boon from his mother, Satyabhama, that everyone should celebrate
2 CONC  his death with colorful light. Thus this day is celebrated as the second day of Diwali - 'Naraka Chaturdashi'. Krishna's and Satyabhama's victory on Narakasura translated into freedom for all his prisoners and honoring of Aditi. 
2 CONC Having rescued the 16,100 women, Krishna married them to restore them to their former dignity. History[edit] Narakasura and his kingdom, Pragjyotisha, find mention in both the Mahabharata and theRamayana, in the sections which wer
2 CONC e written not before the first century. His son, Bhagadatta, is said to have fought for the Kauravas in the Mahabharata battle. Though the boar Prajapati finds mention as early as the Satapatha Brahmana, that the contact with Bhum
2 CONC i engendered a son is first mentioned in the Harivamsa which is assigned to the fifth century. This theme, that of the son Naraka, is further expanded in the later Vishnu Purana. TheBhagavata Purana, which was composed even later,
2 CONC  expands the story even further. The Naraka myth gets the most extensive elaboration in the Upapurana called Kalika Purana (10th century), which was composed in Assam itself. Here the legend of Janaka of Videha, the father ofSita,
2 CONC  is embellished and added to the legend of Naraka. Once Naraka, motivated by his carnal desire, wanted to marry Devi Kamakhya. When proposed, the Goddess playfully put a condition before him that if he would be able to build a sta
2 CONC ircase from the bottom of the Nilachal Hill to the temple within one night before the cock crows to indicate Dawn, then she would surely marry him. Naraka took it as a challenge and tried all with his might to do this huge task. H
2 CONC e was almost about to accomplish the job before it was dawn. When Kamakhya Devi got this news, panic-stricken she strangled a cock and made it crow untimely to give the impression of Dawn to Naraka. Duped by the trick even Naraka 
2 CONC thought that it was a futile job and left it half way through. Later he chased the cock and killed it. Now the place is known as Kukurakata situated in the district of Darrang. The incomplete staircase is known as Mekhelauja Path.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		 	•		 		 	•		 		   
2 CONT
2 CONT •	 How Diwali came to be celebrated – Legend of Narakasura Leave a reply There exists a wonderful story behind how Diwali, the festival of lights came into being celebrated among the people of India.  A story, once again, as a res
2 CONC ult of the illusory puppeteering of Lord Sri Krishna among mortals to restore justice. The beginnings
2 CONT
2 CONT Satyabhama : Krishna’s Warrior Wife (This image may be copyrighted) Source: www.hinduhumanrights.info An asura (non – deva or demon) named Hiranyaksha, tortured the devas of the heavenly abode, drunk with power.  When the devas to
2 CONC ok their plight to Lord Vishnu for help, Hiranyaksha fled and hid himself in the netherworld holding the goddess of Earth, Bhumidevi in captivity.  To find him, Vishnu took the form of a boar (varaha avatar) and plunged into the d
2 CONC epths of the cosmic ocean to the netherworld.
2 CONT  A huge battle ensued between the varaha avatar of Vishnu and Hiranyaksha which lasted for a thousand years,  In the end, Hiranyaksha was decapitated by Vishnu and Bhoomi devi was rescued back out of the cosmic ocean and restored 
2 CONC in all her bounty. Bhoomi devi has a child during the time she was in captivity under Hiranyaksha.  But when rescued by Vishnu, the onseers were misled to think it was the son of Vishnu and Bhoomi devi.  The child was called Bauma
2 CONC n. Bauman, though born from Bhoomi devi, the bountiful giver, was ill-willed and resorted to evil ways of living.  This led some believers to ascertain that he could not have been the son of Vishnu but that of the asura, Hiranyaks
2 CONC ha.
2 CONT
2 CONT Narakasura the evil king Narakasura became the ruler of Jyotispura in Assam which is modern day Guwahati.  He began to adopt evil and sadistic ways of the throne in association with another asura named Banasura.  Fearing that the 
2 CONC people of his reign hated him so much and might turn to a revolt against him, Narakasura meditated towards Lord Brahma and got a boon of invincibility. Drunk with extreme power, he attacked the abode of the devas and took control 
2 CONC of it.  He drove Indra out of his abode and confiscated his white Umbrella (Chhatra chhaayaa) and the earrings of Indra’s mother. People feared to go against him as they believed he was the son of Lord Vishnu himself.  But Brahma 
2 CONC who himself couldn’t tolerate the atrocities of Narakasura, brought this to Vishnu’s notice.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vishnu in perpertual state of harmony (This image may be copyrighted) Source: lh3.googleusercontent.com Even Vishnu plunged into a state of dilemma since Narakasura even had a boon that none other than his very own mother Boomi De
2 CONC vi could kill him.  Yet he reassured Brahma that he will find a way to end Narakasura’s reign of terror.  Then it was time that Lord Vishnu re-incarnated as Sri Krishna in the mortal world.  By his hidden master plan, Narakasura’s
2 CONC  mother Bhumi devi was reincarnated as Satyabama and Krishna married her. Satyabama was adept at the art of charioteering and archery. So Krishna appointed her as his charioteer and attacked Narakasura’s magnificent fortress on hi
2 CONC s vehicle, the garuda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Battle between Krishna and Narakasura A fierce battle ensued and many casualties resulted on both side.  Narakasura put up a valiant defense that even sent fear into the eyes of Satyabama. At one point, Krishna feigned that he had
2 CONC  been fatally wounded by Narakasura’s weapons.  An enraged Satyabama, took out her bow and arrow, with all her might rained bolts over Narakasura which finally killed him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Satyabhama kills Narakasura Only after killing Narakasura, Satyabama realizes that she was an incarnation of Bhoomi devi and that she had killed her very own son Narakasura.  While Narakasura was about to pass away, he asked a boo
2 CONC n from his mother that the day of his death be celebrated with lights all over his kingdom. Satyabama requested her husband Sri Krishna that the day of her son’s death be inferred as Naraka Chaturdashi and be celebrated by lightin
2 CONC g lights all over. Krishna granted her wish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deepavali (Diwali)  From that very day on, to honor the faith in Bhoomi devi that killed her own son to restore justice and peace, and to celebrate the end of a tyrannical rule, people to this day, light deepams (small oil lamps w
2 CONC ith wicks) on the eve of Diwali.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The parents are cited as Hiranyaksha and KAYADU HIRANYAKASHIPU
2 CONT
2 CONT Reference is invited to https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narakasura. Where in it is seen that 
2 CONT
2 CONT ****  In the Srimad Bhagavatam, Narakasura or Naraka is the asura son of the earth goddess Bhudevi (Bhumi) and Varaha (third avatar of Vishnu) and is said to have grown to be a demon through association with Banasura.[1] He is sai
2 CONC d to have established the kingdom of Pragjyotisha after overthrowing the last of the Danava king Ghatakasura. 
