1 OCCU Lord of intellect and communication
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Budha (Sanskrit: बुध) or Saumya (Sanskrit: सौम्य, lit. son of Moon)[1] is the Hindu god of merchandise and the protector of merchants. In Hindu mythology, Budha is also the name and personification of the planet Me
2 CONC rcury. Budha is said to be the son of lunar god Chandra and Tara (the goddess of felicity and the divine consort of Hindu god Brihaspati). His consort is the female form of Hindu androgyne god Ila.
2 CONT
2 CONT Budha is represented as being mild, eloquent, and having a greenish complexion. He is also represented holding a scimitar, a club and a shield, riding a winged lion in Bhudhan Temple. In other illustrations, he holds a sceptre and
2 CONC  lotus and rides a carpet or an eagle or a chariot drawn by lions.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Budha presides over midweek 'Budhavara' or Wednesday. In modern Hindi, Oriya, Telugu, Bengali, Marathi, Urdu, Kannada and Gujarati, Wednesday is called Budhavara; Tamil: Budhan kizhamai; Malayalam: Budhanazhcha; Thai: Wan Phut (วั
2 CONC นพุธ).
2 CONT
2 CONT Budha married Ila, the daughter of Vaivasvata Manu, and fathered a son, Pururava.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth of Budha 
2 CONT
2 CONT Budha with his consort Ila. Budha, a beautiful child, was born to Tara. Chandra claimed to be the father of Budha, but Tara kept silent. Budha himself got angry over this tussle and asked his mother to tell the truth. Tara said th
2 CONC at Chandra was the father of Budha.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Chandra was a student of Brihaspati, the Teacher of devas. Tara was the wife of Brihaspati. Chandra one captured Tara and forcefully married her. Brihaspati became angry and eventually, a war started between Chandra and Brihaspati
2 CONC . Shukracharya, the teacher of the Asuras, joined Chandra in the battle, while The devas joined Brihaspati. As the battle was due to desire for Tara, it was known as Tarakaamayam. Lord Brahma, the creator of the universe, feared t
2 CONC he war might abolish the world. Hence, he came down and stopped the war. He convinced Tara, took her away from Chandra, and gave her back to Brihaspati.
2 CONT
2 CONT Growth of Budha  Chandra gives the child Budha to Rohini and Krittika, two of his wives, to raise. Budha grows up under supervision of Rohini and Krittika. As he grows up, he feels angry and ashamed about his birth. He wants to re
2 CONC ctify the sin of his birth and hence goes to Saravanavana in the Himalayas to do Tapas. Vishnu, pleased with the tapas, shows up to Budha. By Vishnu's blessings Budha learns all the Vedas and arts. However, according to another ve
2 CONC rsion, Budha was raised by Brihaspati and is thus called his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT Temples of Budha Thiruvenkadu in Tamil Nadu which houses the Navagraha temple has a shrine for Budha in the northern end of the Brahmavidyambhika Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT In astrology In Vedic astrology Budha is considered a benefic, unless he is joined with a malefic planet, in which case he becomes malefic also. Mercury rules over Gemini and Virgo, is exalted in Virgo and in his fall in Pisces. B
2 CONC udha is friendly with Saturn and Venus, hostile to the Moon, Mars and Jupiter, and neutral towards the other planets. Budha represents intelligence, intellect, communication, analysis, the senses, science, mathematics, business, e
2 CONC ducation and research. The written word and journeys of all types fall within his domain. Budha is lord of three nakshatras or lunar mansions: Ashlesha, Jyeshtha, and Revati (nakshatra). Budha has the following associations: the c
2 CONC olor green, the metal brass and gemstone emerald.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In Hindu mythology, Budha (Sanskrit: बुध) is the name for the planet Mercury, a son of Chandra (the moon) with Tara or Rohini. He is also the god of merchandise and protector of Merchants.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Budha
