1 NOTE {geni:about_me} PURURAVA  WAS VERY VIRTIOUS KING AND BECAME ONE OF THE SIX WORLD MONARCHS. HE ESTABLISHED THE CITY OF PRAYAG WHICH LATER BECAME THE CAPITAL OF HIS KINGDOM.PURURAVA HELPED INDRA TO DEFEAT MIGHTY DEMONKESHI AND ATTRA
2 CONC CTED BY HIS VIRTUES,  URVASHI APPROACHED HIM WITH A DESIRE TO MARRY HIM. PURURAVA WELCOMED URVASHI AND TOGETHER THEY BEGET SIX SONS. AYU, SHRUTAYU, RAY, VIJAY AND JAY.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pururavas (Devanagari:पुरूरव) was the first king of the Aila dynasty or the Somavamsha. According to theVedas, he is a mythological entity associated with the Surya (the sun) and Usha (the dawn), and is believed to resided in the 
2 CONC middle region of the cosmos. The Rig Veda (X.95.18) states that he was a son of Ila[1] and was a pious king. However, the Mahabharata states that Ila was both his mother and his father. According to the Vishnu Purana, his father w
2 CONC as Budha, and he was ancestor of the tribe of Pururavas, from whom descended the Kauravas andPandavas. •	 Narrative of Urvashi and Pururavas [edit] The earlier version of the narrative of Urvashi and Pururavas is found in the Rigv
2 CONC eda (X.95.1-18) and theŚatapaṭha Brāhmaṇa (XI.5.1). The later versions are found in the Mahābhārata, the Harivaṃsa, theViṣṇu Purāṇa, the Matsya Purāṇa,[2] and the Bhāgavata Purāṇa. In Vedic literature [edit] The Ṛg-veda, X.129 con
2 CONC tains a conversational fragment, written in a highly wrought poetic style. The hymn suggests that Uṣas (also known as Urvaśi) is a Gandharvi or Apsara (an aquatic nymph). Having been united with a human king, Purūravas, and after 
2 CONC living together for four autumns, suddenly left him on his unintentional violation of the stipulated conditions of the union. Later Purūravas made futile entreaties to her to return to him.[2] The narrative displays multiple level
2 CONC s of symbolism by playing on the multiplicity of meanings in the Vedic Saṃskṛt terms. While it is a love poem, expressing the conflict of interest between a lover and his beloved, who spurns his love, it also expresses the immorta
2 CONC l relationship between the Sun (Purūravas) and the Dawn (Uṣas). In addition to these two levels of meaning, it also offers mantric prescriptions for a ritual activity bent on taking rebirth as a Gandharva or Apsaras. In classical 
2 CONC Sanskrit literature [edit] The love story of king Pururavas and celestial nymph Urvashi is found in the Sanskrit drama,Vikramōrvaśīyam, written by the celebrated poet Kalidasa. Life and descendants [edit] According to the Puranas,
2 CONC  Pururavas reigned from Pratisthana (Prayaga). He had six (or seven or eight according to different accounts) sons. The names of these sons are, Ayu (or Ayus), Amavasu,[3]Vishvayu, Shrutayu, Shatayu and Dridhayu. Nahusha, the son 
2 CONC of Ayu, is a well-known da[4]
2 CONT
