1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ulupi''' (also known as Uloopi), is a character in the Hindu epic Mahabharata. The daughter of Kauravya, the king of Nāgas, she was among the four wives of Arjuna. She also finds a mention in the Vishnu Purana a
2 CONC nd the Bhagavata Purana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulupi is said to have met and married Arjuna when he was in exile, and with whom she bore his son Irāvān. She played a major part in the upbringing of Babruvāhana, Arjuna's son with Chitrāngadā. She is also credited with redeeming
2 CONC  Arjuna from the curse of the Vasus by restoring his life after he was slain in a battle by Babruvāhana.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulupi
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Affiliation	Nāga
2 CONT * Family	Kauravya (father), Vishvahini (mother), Kunti (mother-in-law), Pandavas (brothers-in-law), Draupadi, Subhadra, Chitrāngadā
2 CONT * Spouse	Arjuna
2 CONT * Children	Irāvān
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT The Story of Uloopi and Arjuna – Ulupi – the Naga Princess
2 CONT
2 CONT Uloopi was a Naga Princess and she got married to Arjuna, the third of the Pandava brothers. She was the daughter of the Serpent King, Kauravya, who ruled the underwater kingdom of snakes in the GangaRiver. Ulupi was an expert war
2 CONC rior and she taught the art of warfare to Babruvahan, Arjuna's son by his another wife, Chitrangada. Uloopi brought back Arjuna to life after he was killed by Babruvahan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Arjuna Goes on Exile for a Year and Meets Ulupi
2 CONT
2 CONT It is mentioned in the Mahabharata that Arjuna was once forced to break the rule of not entering the palace ofPanchali – the wife of the Pandavas. As Panchali, or Draupadi, was married to the five Pandava brothers, they had agreed
2 CONC  that one Pandava brother will spend a year with Panchali and the other four will not enter her palace during the period. If the rule is broken, then the one who breaks the rule will go on an exile for a year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arjuna had broken this rule once as he had forgotten his bow and arrow (Gandiva) in the palace of Draupadi and he needed them to help a poor man. So he broke the rule and had to go on exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage of Arjuna and Uloopi
2 CONT
2 CONT It was during this exile period that Arjuna met Uloopi, the Naga Princess. Ulupi was impressed by Arjuna and took him to under water world. Here she convinced Arjuna to marry her. A son named Iravan was born to them. Ulupi also ga
2 CONC ve the boon to Arjuna that all animals live in the water will obey him and he will be invincible under the water.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arjuna is Killed by Babruvahan and Uloopi Brings Him Back to Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Years after when Pandavas performed the Ashvamedha sacrifice, the sacrificial horse was stopped by Babruvahan when it entered his territory. Babruvahan was taught the art of warfare of Ulupi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Babruvahan was not aware that Arjuna was his father and he fought the army led by Arjuna which was following the sacrificial horse. During the course of the battle, Babruvahan’s arrow hit Arjun and he fell unconscious.
2 CONT
2 CONT Uloopi who came to know about the incident rushed to the spot and saved the life of Arjuna using the knowledge only available to the Nagas. Later Uloopi united the father and son – Babruvahan and Arjuna.
2 CONT
2 CONT The above incident is connected to yet another legend – it is said that Ganga Devi cursed Arjuna that he will be killed by his son as he had treacherously killed Bhishma in the battle of Kurukshetra by placing Shikandi (Eunuch) as
2 CONC  a shield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulupi who came to know about this curse prayed to Goddess Ganga for forgiveness. Ganga Devi told Ulupi that Babruvahan will kill Arjuna but she will be able to bring him back to life with the help of Mritasanjivani. When the Panda
2 CONC vas started their final journey, Uloopi is said to have to returned to the under water kingdom in the Ganga River.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulupi While Arjuna was in Manipur, Ulupi, a Naga princess of otherwise noble character, became infatuated of him. She caused him to be abducted after he had been intoxicated with potent concoctions; she had him conveyed to her rea
2 CONC lm in the netherworld. Here, Ulupi induced an unwilling Arjuna to take her for wife. Later, the large-hearted Ulupi restored Arjuna to the lamenting Chitrangada. Ulupi later did much to further the comfort and happiness not only o
2 CONC f Arjuna, but also of Chitrangada and the young Babruvahana. She played a very major part in the upbringing of Babruvahana; she enjoyed much influence over him, and was eventually also to restore Arjuna to life after he was slain 
2 CONC in battle by Babruvahana.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pandava brothers followed the instructions provided by Krishna (Vyasa in some retellings) for sharing Draupadi equally. Each brother used to stay with Draupadi for a year and then wait for another four years to get the turn ag
2 CONC ain. During this time, none of the others were to go to Draupadi's chamber out of turn. If anyone breaks this rule, then he will have to live in exile.
2 CONT
2 CONT One day Arjuna entered Draupadi's chamber to retrieve his weapons when she was with Yuddhistira. Arjuna was trying to protect the cattle of a brahmin from some thieves. Then he rightfully accepted the rule of exile. Arjuna gave im
2 CONC portance to his Kshatriya dharma (warrior code) over the other family law.
2 CONT
2 CONT During this exile, Arjuna went to pilgrimage centers. He had a fling with Ulupi (naga woman) and fathered a child called Iravan. Arjuna gradually forgot this as well. Arjuna also married a great warrior woman called Chitrangada in
2 CONC  Manipur. He visited Dwaraka and stayed with Shri Krishna for some time. Ulupi
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulūpī or Uloopi, in the Hindu epic Mahabharata, was one of Arjuna's wives. While Arjuna was in Manipur, the Naga princess became infatuated with him. She caused him to be abducted after he had been intoxicated with potent concocti
2 CONC ons and had him conveyed to her realm in the netherworld. There, Ulūpī induced an unwilling Arjuna to take her for a wife. She was the mother of Iravan. She later restored Arjuna to the lamenting Chitrāngadā, one of Arjuna's other
2 CONC  wives. She played a major part in the upbringing of Arjuna and Chitrangada's son, Babruvahana. She was also able to restore Arjuna to life after he was slain in battle by Babruvahana. When Arjuna was given a curse by the Vasus,Bh
2 CONC eeshma's brothers after he killed Bheeshma in the Kurushtra war,She redeemed him Arjuna from his curse.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Belongs to a Naga tribe in the Himalayan region.
