1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Kaikeyi is King Dasaratha's second wife, the mother of Bharata. Manthara is her evil, conniving attendant. When Manthara learns that the throne is to be given to Rama, she plants the seeds of jealousy in Kaikeyi's mind and convinc
2 CONC es her to use the two boons granted her by the King to force him to instead place Bharata on the throne and exile Rama to the forest for 14 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaikeyi--==
2 CONT 	 Kaikeyi (Sanskrit: कैकेयी, Kaikeyī, Indonesian: Kekayi, Burmese: Kaike, Malay: Kekayi, Thai: Kaiyakesi, Khmer: Kaikesi), in the Hindu epic Rāmāyaṇa, was the last of King Daśaratha's three wives and a queen of Ayodhyā. She was th
2 CONC e mother of Bharata. The term Kaikeyī in Sanskrit means "belonging to Kekaya Kingdom", referring the ruling family of the Kekaya clan, to whom Kaikeyī belonged. •	 Early life The daughter of the mighty Ashwapati, a long-term ally 
2 CONC of Kosala, Kaikeyi married Dasaratha after the latter had promised her father that the son born of her womb would succeed him as King of Kosala. Dasaratha was able to make this promise as his first wife, Kausalya, was childless an
2 CONC d not likely to produce a son of her own. Kaikeyi also remained barren for many years of marriage, as a result of which Dasaratha married Sumitra, the princess of Magadha, another kingdom with strong political ties to Kosala. Kaik
2 CONC eyi's personality is worth examining and provides a strong clue to her motivations which later led to her insisting on the exile of her stepson from Ayodhya. As a young girl and the only sister to seven brothers, Kaikeyi grew up w
2 CONC ithout a maternal influence in her childhood home. Her father had banished her mother from Kekaya after realizing that his wife's nature was not conducive to a happy family life. Amongst other things, due to a boon, Ashwapati was 
2 CONC able to understand the language of the birds. However, this was accompanied by a caveat that if he ever revealed the content of bird speak to anyone, even his own mother, that he would forthwith lose his life. One day, the King an
2 CONC d his Queen were strolling through the palace gardens when Ashwapati happened to overhear the conversation of a pair of mated swans. The conversation so amused him that he laughed heartily, instigating his wife's curiosity. Despit
2 CONC e being aware of the fact that he could not divulge the content of the conversation to her, without losing his life, Kaikeyi's mother insisted on knowing the cause of the King's mirth. When Ashwapati realized that his wife cared l
2 CONC ittle for his life or well-being, he had her banished to her parents' home. Kaikeyi never saw her mother again. She was raised by her wet nurse, Manthara, who accompanied Kaikeyi to Ayodhya as a trusted maid upon her marriage to D
2 CONC asaratha. Her father's treatment of her mother and the latter's subsequent exile led to Kaikeyi harboring a deep distrust of men in general and husbands in particular, and to considering their love as "fickle" and "passing" in nat
2 CONC ure. In addition, she was very insecure in her position as secondary consort to Dasaratha. She realized that Dasarathadeeply respected his Queen and Empress, Kausalya, and had only married her in order to produce the much-needed h
2 CONC eir. To this end, Kaikeyi realized that her position in her husband's affections and esteem relied heavily on her ability to produce that heir. When she remained barren, she became increasingly insecure and realized that she could
2 CONC  never win in her struggle for supremacy over Kausalya, although Manthara proved to be a great help in this regard. The older woman schemed constantly to further her own position at the Court. And since her position depended on Ka
2 CONC ikeyi's status at Court, Manthara lost no opportunity to feed the young Kaikeyi's insecurity and jealousy of Kaushalya, despite Dasaratha's obvious enchantment and love for all of his wives. Manthara's scheming paid off when Kaike
2 CONC yi was able to convince her husband to take her along with him during a military campaign against Samhasura, an enemy of both Indra and Dasaratha. During a fierce battle between the two, the wheel of Daśaratha's chariot broke and 
2 CONC Samhasura's arrow pierced the King's armor and lodged in his chest. Kaikeyi, who was acting as Dasaratha's charioteer, quickly repaired the broken wheel and then drove the chariot away from the battle field. She nursed the wounded
2 CONC  King back to health. Touched by her courage and timely service, Daśaratha offered her two boons. However, Kaikeyī chose to ask those boons later. Boon
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaikeyi demands that Dasaratha banquish Rama from Ayodhya Years passed and all three Queens produced sons. Rama, the son of Kausalya, was Dasaratha's favorite son. Rama was a loving, obedient child who followed his father's foot s
2 CONC teps. When he turned 16 and was to be crowned King, Kaikeyi was delighted and as happy as she would have been had it been her own son, Bharata's, coronation. However, Manthara, worried that Kaikeyi would lose her status as Chief Q
2 CONC ueen at Court if Rama ascended the throne (making Kausalya the Queen Mother) decided to instigate trouble. She fueled Kaikeyi's dormant jealousy and envy of Kausalya, reminded her that her son's coronation would give Kausalya her 
2 CONC former status as the most important of Dasaratha's Queens and would cut Bharata out of the royal lineage forever. Finally, Kaikeyi's ardent desire to retain superior status over Kausalya motivated her to demand the two boons grant
2 CONC ed to her years earlier by Dasaratha and to further remind him of his promise to Ashwapati that the son born of her (Kaikeyi's) womb should succeed Dasaratha as King of Kosala. In order to ensure that Rama would be no threat to he
2 CONC r son, Kaikeyi further demanded the exile of Rama from Ayodhya for 14 long years, reasoning that this length of time would be enough for Bharata to consolidate his position as King of Kosala.[1] But Kaikeyī's desire never bore fru
2 CONC it. After sending his son into exile, a grief-striken Dasaratha died of a broken heart six days after Rama left Ayodhya. She came to blame herself for this death. Furthermore, Bharata swore never to ascend the throne as it was his
2 CONC  older brother's birth right. He further blamed her for his father's death and is said never to have addressed her as "mother" again. Kaikeyi was said to have died a lonely and broken-hearted woman, estranged from her son, his wif
2 CONC e (the cousin of Rama's wife, Sita) and their two sons, her only grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Kaikeyi (Sanskrit: कैकेयी, Kaikeyī, Burmese: Kaike, Malay: Kekayi, Thai: Kaiyakesi, Khmer: Kaikesi), in the Hindu epic Rāmāyaṇa, was the second of King Daśaratha's three wives and a queen of Ayodhyā. She was the mother of Bharata.
2 CONC  The term Kaikeyī in Sanskrit means "belonging to the Kaikeyas", referring the ruling family of the Kekaya clan, to whom Kaikeyī belonged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT The daughter of the mighty Ashwapati, a long-term ally of Kosala, Kaikeyi married Dasaratha after the latter had promised her father that the son born of her womb would succeed him as King of Kosala. Dasaratha was able to make thi
2 CONC s promise as his first wife, Kausalya, was childless and not likely to produce a son of her own. Kaikeyi also remained barren for many years of marriage, as a result of which Dasaratha married Sumitra, the princess of Magadha, ano
2 CONC ther kingdom with strong political ties to Kosala.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaikeyi's personality is worth examining and provides a strong clue to her motivations which later led to her insisting on the exile of her stepson from Ayodhya. As a young girl and the only sister to seven brothers, Kaikeyi grew 
2 CONC up without a maternal influence in her childhood home. Her father had banished her mother from Kekaya after realizing that his wife's nature was not conducive to a happy family life. Amongst other things, due to a boon, Ashwapati 
2 CONC was able to understand the language of the birds. However, this was accompanied by a caveat that if he ever revealed the content of bird speak to anyone, even his own mother, that he would forthwith lose his life. One day, the Kin
2 CONC g and his Queen were strolling through the palace gardens when Ashwapati happened to overhear the conversation of a pair of mated swans. The conversation so amused him that he laughed heartily, instigating his wife's curiosity. De
2 CONC spite being aware of the fact that he could not divulge the content of the conversation to her, without losing his life, Kaikeyi's mother insisted on knowing the cause of the King's mirth. When Ashwapati realized that his wife car
2 CONC ed little for his life or well-being, he had her banished to her parents' home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaikeyi never saw her mother again. She was raised by her wet nurse, Manthara, who accompanied Kaikeyi to Ayodhya as a trusted maid upon her marriage to Dasaratha. Her father's treatment of her mother and the latter's subsequent e
2 CONC xile led to Kaikeyi harboring a deep distrust of men in general and husbands in particular, and to considering their love as "fickle" and "passing" in nature. In addition, she was very insecure in her position as secondary consort
2 CONC  to Dasaratha. She realized that Dasaratha deeply respected his Queen and Empress, Kausalya, and had only married her in order to produce the much-needed heir. To this end, Kaikeyi realized that her position in her husband's affec
2 CONC tions and esteem relied heavily on her ability to produce that heir. When she remained barren, she became increasingly insecure and realized that she could never win in her struggle for supremacy over Kausalya, although Manthara p
2 CONC roved to be a great help in this regard. The older woman schemed constantly to further her own position at the Court. And since her position depended on Kaikeyi's status at Court, Manthara lost no opportunity to feed the young Kai
2 CONC keyi's insecurity and jealousy of Kausalya, despite Dasaratha's obvious enchantment and love for all of his wives. It is important to point out that other sources state that Kaikeyi was not insecure, that she loved her other Queen
2 CONC  sisters, and was the backbone of the group. She had saved King Dasaratha in battle and demonstrated her warrior courage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Manthara's scheming paid off when Kaikeyi was able to convinced her husband to take her along with him during a military campaign against Samhasura, an enemy of both Indra and Dasaratha. During a fierce battle between the two, the
2 CONC  wheel of Daśaratha's chariot broke and Samhasura's arrow pierced the King's armor and lodged in his chest. Kaikeyi, who was acting as Dasaratha's charioteer, quickly repaired the broken wheel and then drove the chariot away from 
2 CONC the battle field. She nursed the wounded King back to health. Touched by her courage and timely service, Daśaratha offered her two boons. However, Kaikeyī chose to ask those boons later. In addition, she became his favorite wife a
2 CONC nd finally gained ascendancy over Kausalya.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reason why Manthara was accompanied Kaikeyi to Ayodhya
2 CONT
2 CONT As king Ashwapati was able to understand the language of the birds, he came to know that in near future there would be the dangerous of many evil kings, who could destroy the entire humanity and also about the avatar of lord Rama.
2 CONC  King Dasaratha loved Rama so much that he won’t allow Rama to go to the dense jungle and kill rakshasas who are wrecking the tapasya and sacrifices of brahmins and other humans of entire India. So king Ashwapati told this and ord
2 CONC ered the manthara to send the Rama in the jungles to save the lives of many innocents. Lord Rama was completely aware of the entire incidence. Therefore while returning back from Lanka, he first met and touched the feet of mata Ka
2 CONC ikeyi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Boon
2 CONT
2 CONT Years passed and all three Queens produced sons. Rama, the son of Kausalya, was Dasaratha's favorite son. Rama was a loving, obedient child who followed his father's foot steps and revered Kaikeyi over his own mother, leading to t
2 CONC he former's deep love and affection for him. When he turned 16 and was to be crowned King, Kaikeyi was delighted and as happy as she would have been had it been her own son, Bharata's, coronation. However, Manthara, worried that K
2 CONC aikeyi would lose her status as Chief Queen at Court if Rama ascended the throne (making Kausalya the Queen Mother) decided to instigate trouble She fueled Kaikeyi's dormant jealousy and envy of Kausalya, reminded her that her son
2 CONC 's coronation would give Kausalya her former status as the most important of Dasaratha's Queens and would cut Bharata out of the royal lineage for ever. Finally, Kaikeyi's ardent desire to retain superior status over Kausalya moti
2 CONC vated her to demand the two boons granted to her years earlier by Dasaratha and to further remind him of his promise to Ashwapati that the son born of her (Kaikeyi's) womb should succeed Dasaratha as King of Kosala. In order to en
2 CONC sure that Rama would be no threat to her son, Kaikeyi further demanded the exile of Rama from Ayodhya for 14 long years, reasoning that this length of time would be enough for Bharata to consolidate his position as King of Kosala.
2 CONC [1].
2 CONT
2 CONT But Kaikeyī's desire never bore fruit. After sending his son into exile, a grief-striken Dasaratha died of a broken heart six days after Rama left Ayodhya. She came to blame herself for this death. Furthermore, Bharata swore never
2 CONC  to ascend the throne as it was his older brother's birth right. He further blamed her for his father's death and is said never to have addressed her as "Mother" again. Kaikeyi was said to have died a lonely and broken-hearted wom
2 CONC an, estranged from her son, his wife (the cousin of Rama's wife, Sita) and their two sons, her only grandchildren.
2 CONT
