2 CONT Shibi (king)
2 CONT
2 CONT Sibi offering flesh to Eagle to protect Pigeon King Sibi Chakravarti (Emperor Sibi) is a famous king inHindu mythology and Buddhist Jataka Tales. Sibi was the son of Usinara a famous king of Chandravamsa (Lunar dynasty). He was re
2 CONC nowned for his liberality and unselfishness and is said to have saved Agni (transformed into a dove) from Indra(transformed into a hawk) by offering his own flesh.[1] His lineage is traced from Vishnu in the following order;[2] Br
2 CONC ahmā-Atri-Candra-Buda-Purūravas-Āyus-Nahusa-Yayāti-Anudruhyu-Sabhānara-Kālanara-Srñjya-Usinara-Sibi The story of the King and the pigeon and the hawk is used to illustrate the compassion and generosity of the king. This story of S
2 CONC ibi appears in both the Mahabharata and theRamayana. The story of Sibi in brief is as follows: The Gods, wanting to test the compassionate nature of King Sibi, took the form of a hawk and a pigeon. The pigeon chased by the hawk fe
2 CONC ll on King Sibi's lap seeking his protection. The hawk argued that the pigeon is its food. Sibi offered to compensate with his own flesh. Finally he offered himself to be eaten and the Gods showed him their true form and blessed h
2 CONC im. The king is found among other legendary kings in the lineage claimed by the later Chola kings. The Cholas claimed to have descended from the Sun. The Tamil name for Sibi Chakravarthi is Sembiyanand the Chola kings took this as
2 CONC  one of their titles. There are also a number of temples in Tamil Nadu with legends claiming King Sibi to have worshipped in them. Sibi is considered the King of a country called Ushinara.
